The profiling data shows the timer overhead is substantial—about 18 milliseconds out of the 42 milliseconds total, driven by the innermost timers being called tens of thousands of times each and performing repeated map lookups and vector operations for every invocation.

The key insight is that the destructor's bookkeeping operations (updating the timing map, incrementing counters, managing child_time and children vectors) happen after the end time is captured, so they don't get charged to the child timer's own duration. Instead, all that overhead gets attributed to the parent's self-time since the parent's timer is still running while the child does its cleanup work. This explains why the parent's self-time appears inflated—it's absorbing the cost of managing all its children's timer records. So roughly 157 nanoseconds per timer operation, which tracks with the string-keyed map lookups. The real insight is that the 11.55ms of unaccounted self-time in clip_loop isn't hidden algorithmic work—it's the timer instrumentation overhead itself. Each of those 114k+ timer destructions performs multiple map operations that get attributed to the parent's self-time rather than the children, while the actual loop work (dead checks, swaps) is negligible. The 18ms difference between running with and without timers confirms this is pure measurement cost.

The 96ms spike is just a scheduling artifact, so I have what I need to present the answer.Confirmed on both counts. Here's what's going on.

## 1. Yes, `get_conv_from_grid` is computed once and translated

The current code builds the hull only when `(EPSILON, DELTA)` changes, then reuses it:

```407:430:simplify_geometry.h
inline std::vector<Point> get_conv_from_grid(const Point& p, double EPSILON, double DELTA) {
    thread_local double cached_eps   = std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN();
    thread_local double cached_delta = std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN();
    thread_local std::vector<std::array<double, 2>> hull_offsets;

    if (EPSILON != cached_eps || DELTA != cached_delta) {
        std::vector<Point> points = get_points_from_grid(Point(0, 0), EPSILON, DELTA);
        // ... convex_hull_2 once, store offsets ...
    }
    // every call: just translate the cached hull by p
    for (const auto& off : hull_offsets)
        conv.emplace_back(px + off[0], py + off[1]);
```

For a full run the parameters never change, so the hull is built exactly once and all 5,616 calls hit the cheap translate path. That's the 6.84 ms → ~0.4 ms drop.

## 2. The remaining 30% is mostly the timer overhead itself, not hidden algorithmic work

This is the key insight. I measured the core with timers **off** vs **on**:

- Timers **off**: ~24 ms (24.65, 24.09, 23.45)
- Timers **on**: ~42 ms (42.24, 41.55, and one 96 ms scheduling outlier)

So the instrumentation adds **~18 ms of pure overhead** — nearly half the "timed" total. That overhead doesn't vanish; it lands in the **parent's self-time**. Look at the `Timer` destructor:

```52:65:timer.h
    ~Timer() {
        if (!active) return;
        auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
        auto dur = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(end - start).count();
        timing()[name] += dur;                    // std::map<string> lookup
        timer_detail::counters()[name]++;          // std::map<string> lookup
        auto& stack = timer_detail::stack();
        if (stack.size() >= 2) {
            const char* parent = stack[stack.size() - 2];
            timer_detail::child_time()[parent] += dur;      // another map lookup
            timer_detail::children()[parent].push_back(name); // vector push
        }
        stack.pop_back();
    }
```

Each child timer captures its `end` time *before* doing those four string-keyed `std::map` operations. So a child's bookkeeping cost is **excluded from the child's own duration but included in the parent's**, because the parent's timer is still running. `find_F` and `intersect` each run 57,318 times — that's **114,636 timer destructors**, each doing ~4 `std::map<std::string,...>` lookups. All of that bookkeeping gets charged to `stab.clip_loop`'s self-time.

That's exactly your 11.55 ms of `stab.clip_loop` self-time. It is **not** untracked geometry work — the real loop bookkeeping is tiny (`stab.scan` 0.11 ms, `stab.commit` 0.17 ms, and the `dead[]` checks are trivial). It's the measurement apparatus.

### The takeaway

The profiler is a [Heisenberg](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Observer_effect_(physics)) situation: the more finely you subdivide the hot loop, the more the timer overhead inflates the parent self-time, so the percentages under a heavily-instrumented node will *never* close to 100% with real work. The uninstrumented core is ~24 ms; the ~42 ms timed number is inflated by the ~18 ms of timing overhead that pools into the parent self-times.

If you want the self-time gap to actually reflect real work, the fix is to make the innermost timers cheaper — e.g. accumulate into an integer index / thread-local counters instead of `std::map<std::string>` lookups per call, or drop the per-call `find_F`/`intersect` timers and rely on sampling (Instruments/`perf`) for that level. Want me to switch the timer to an interned-index accumulator so the per-call overhead nearly disappears?

